101
00:04:02,120 --> 00:04:03,440
 - So let me just reframe it.

102
00:04:03,440 --> 00:04:06,080
 So the promise of stable coins, right?

103
00:04:06,080 --> 00:04:06,920
 What are stable coins?

104
00:04:06,920 --> 00:04:09,160
 They are blockchain-based systems

105
00:04:09,160 --> 00:04:12,720
 where you layer on dollars and other kind of quote-unquote

106
00:04:12,720 --> 00:04:15,280
 fiat money on top of these systems.

107
00:04:15,280 --> 00:04:16,480
 The simple way I've been explaining

108
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:19,040
 like when I talked to non-tech folks,

109
00:04:19,040 --> 00:04:20,680
 for example, the policymakers,

110
00:04:20,680 --> 00:04:23,720
 the simple way I explain blockchains is

111
00:04:23,720 --> 00:04:25,000
 they're technologies that allow you

112
00:04:25,000 --> 00:04:28,240
 to build digital services that don't have intermediaries.

113
00:04:28,240 --> 00:04:29,680
 That's a little bit simplified,

114
00:04:29,680 --> 00:04:30,720
 but it directionally,

115
00:04:30,720 --> 00:04:32,440
 I think a very useful way to think of it.

116
00:04:32,440 --> 00:04:34,400
 So that what that means is you can,

117
00:04:34,400 --> 00:04:36,760
 if you do it right, build services

118
00:04:36,760 --> 00:04:40,360
 that function very similarly to how our payment system works,

119
00:04:40,360 --> 00:04:41,920
 but you don't have these companies

120
00:04:41,920 --> 00:04:44,480
 like Visa and payment processors and so forth.

121
00:04:44,480 --> 00:04:46,360
 In the middle, why is that important?

122
00:04:46,360 --> 00:04:48,200
 Because by putting it in the middle,

123
00:04:48,200 --> 00:04:51,200
 you're giving them power and that power lets them take money

124
00:04:51,200 --> 00:04:54,200
 and also lets them kind of wield control.

125
00:04:54,200 --> 00:04:56,040
 So sort of two dimensions, there's financial

126
00:04:56,040 --> 00:04:58,440
 and then kind of governance control.

127
00:04:58,440 --> 00:05:00,520
 The financial one, I think, is the easiest to focus on

128
00:05:00,520 --> 00:05:03,440
 because it's so visible and you can clearly improve that.

129
00:05:03,440 --> 00:05:05,600
 And it's significant, I mean, two and a half percent.

130
00:05:05,600 --> 00:05:06,520
 You know, we're in the tech world,

131
00:05:06,520 --> 00:05:08,320
 we're used to these 70% margin businesses.

132
00:05:08,320 --> 00:05:10,280
 Most of the world is much lower margin.

133
00:05:10,280 --> 00:05:11,920
 - And we did that analysis for grocery stores

134
00:05:11,920 --> 00:05:14,400
 where it's like a grocery store is making 2% profit.

135
00:05:14,400 --> 00:05:16,280
 We're talking about doubling profitability

136
00:05:16,280 --> 00:05:17,800
 of a grocery store.

137
00:05:17,800 --> 00:05:21,640
 There is this triple regressive tax going on here too

138
00:05:21,640 --> 00:05:24,640
 where the biggest retailers in the US

139
00:05:24,640 --> 00:05:26,720
 are negotiating custom rates

140
00:05:26,720 --> 00:05:28,520
 that are maybe 10 times cheaper, you know,

141
00:05:28,520 --> 00:05:31,280
 10% of the fee that your local grocery store pay,

142
00:05:31,280 --> 00:05:33,160
 Kroger's like paying much less than that.

143
00:05:33,160 --> 00:05:38,000
 And the wealthiest users get these rewards back.

144
00:05:38,000 --> 00:05:41,000
 And so in many ways, the average consumer

145
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:43,480
 is really getting screwed by these extractive fees.

146
00:05:43,480 --> 00:05:45,640
 - This is truly scandalous and it's something

147
00:05:45,640 --> 00:05:47,200
 that people don't think enough about.

148
00:05:47,200 --> 00:05:49,560
 It's really the deck is stacked against the little guy

149
00:05:49,560 --> 00:05:50,400
 in this situation.

150
00:05:50,400 --> 00:05:51,360
 - It's true in all finance.

151
00:05:51,360 --> 00:05:54,160
 Like, you know, if you have a lot of money in savings,

152
00:05:54,160 --> 00:05:55,600
 you go and put them in treasury bills

153
00:05:55,600 --> 00:05:57,800
 and you get 4% and chase, you get 0%.

154
00:05:57,800 --> 00:06:00,520
 If you want to cash or paycheck, you know,

155
00:06:00,520 --> 00:06:02,960
 if you have money, it's effectively free.

156
00:06:02,960 --> 00:06:04,320
 And if you don't, you do pay a loan.

157
00:06:04,320 --> 00:06:06,960
 So like all of this stuff is highly regressive.

158
00:06:06,960 --> 00:06:09,040
 Okay, so there's the removing intermediaries.

159
00:06:09,040 --> 00:06:11,000
 One thing you do is you lower fees.

160
00:06:11,000 --> 00:06:14,360
 The other thing you do is you are essentially building these

161
00:06:14,360 --> 00:06:17,040
 as, you know, I think of it as, instead of being dependent

162
00:06:17,040 --> 00:06:20,640
 on Visa and their APIs and all of their other kinds of things.

163
00:06:20,640 --> 00:06:22,880
 Now you have a thousand companies with different APIs

164
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:26,080
 and who knows what the rules are building on top of them.

165
00:06:26,080 --> 00:06:28,240
 I think if you go look inside of these payment processor

166
00:06:28,240 --> 00:06:30,800
 companies like Stripe, they have these massive teams

167
00:06:30,800 --> 00:06:33,680
 that are going and dealing with all of these special cases.

168
00:06:33,680 --> 00:06:35,560
 And then that's what makes the businesses valuable

169
00:06:35,560 --> 00:06:38,040
 is that they've done so much kind of grind work.

170
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:40,520
 So if you imagine a world where you have sort of

171
00:06:40,520 --> 00:06:43,360
 this blockchain based infrastructure and the ideal case,

172
00:06:43,360 --> 00:06:45,560
 you have this unified global network payments become

173
00:06:45,560 --> 00:06:48,520
 like email in the web or it's just sort of this global protocol

174
00:06:48,520 --> 00:06:50,440
 and you're running, you know, I'm sending you dollars

175
00:06:50,440 --> 00:06:53,040
 or euros and you have these compliance

176
00:06:53,040 --> 00:06:56,320
 and charge back services on top kind of bundled.

177
00:06:56,320 --> 00:06:59,360
 Then you have this sort of, you know, universal global APIs

178
00:06:59,360 --> 00:07:00,560
 is what we call composability.

179
00:07:00,560 --> 00:07:04,280
 You can build services on top that a relatively small company

180
00:07:04,280 --> 00:07:06,720
 without this giant integration teams can build a service

181
00:07:06,720 --> 00:07:08,760
 that's like a fraud service or a charge back service.

182
00:07:08,760 --> 00:07:11,440
 Or, you know, like, look, a big, big problem right now

183
00:07:11,440 --> 00:07:12,400
 is invoice fraud.

184
00:07:12,400 --> 00:07:14,920
 Most businesses right there sending out a lot of them

185
00:07:14,920 --> 00:07:16,360
 are sending out millions of dollars a day

186
00:07:16,360 --> 00:07:19,080
 and they're basically getting a PDF or a piece of paper

187
00:07:19,080 --> 00:07:22,120
 and it says you owe me $100,000 and they've checked the address

188
00:07:22,120 --> 00:07:24,440
 and you'll see all these notices now, like,

189
00:07:24,440 --> 00:07:25,440
 make sure you call them.

190
00:07:25,440 --> 00:07:27,160
 So like the best practice now is to actually,

191
00:07:27,160 --> 00:07:28,240
 like, literally pick up the phone

192
00:07:28,240 --> 00:07:30,080
 and make sure you hear the person's voice and...

193
00:07:30,080 --> 00:07:32,240
 - Very efficient, very efficient process.

194
00:07:32,240 --> 00:07:33,160
 - Really that secure.

195
00:07:33,160 --> 00:07:35,520
 I mean, in a world where we're all carrying around these,

196
00:07:35,520 --> 00:07:39,360
 you know, supercomputers that have perfect facial detection

197
00:07:39,360 --> 00:07:42,520
 and cryptography, like it just seems kind of strange, right?

198
00:07:42,520 --> 00:07:44,240
 And so, you know, these companies like Stripe,

199
00:07:44,240 --> 00:07:46,760
 because they've kind of done what a blockchain would do

200
00:07:46,760 --> 00:07:50,840
 and built this integration layer with all of these services,

201
00:07:50,840 --> 00:07:52,480
 they can then start to build, essentially,

202
00:07:52,480 --> 00:07:54,360
 reputation services on top that say,

203
00:07:54,360 --> 00:07:56,800
 okay, this sender is valid and this one is, you know,

204
00:07:56,800 --> 00:07:59,320
 like a whitelist approach, the way you do with email.

205
00:07:59,320 --> 00:08:02,320
 But if you had this global blockchain payment system,

206
00:08:02,320 --> 00:08:04,440
 you could imagine a world where then all these people

207
00:08:04,440 --> 00:08:06,520
 are building lots of startups and big companies

208
00:08:06,520 --> 00:08:08,360
 are building essentially reputation services

209
00:08:08,360 --> 00:08:09,720
 and other kinds of things around it,

210
00:08:09,720 --> 00:08:12,000
 which I think would have all these secondary benefits

211
00:08:12,000 --> 00:08:15,360
 beyond just the fee reduction, right, it would reduce fraud,

212
00:08:15,360 --> 00:08:17,280
 make it more reliable, you could build reputation systems.

213
00:08:17,280 --> 00:08:19,120
 You could do all the things you'd done on the internet.

214
00:08:19,120 --> 00:08:20,560
 Like you think about email as a protocol,

215
00:08:20,560 --> 00:08:22,680
 we now have all these interesting services on top

216
00:08:22,680 --> 00:08:24,960
 because it was a global open protocol.

217
00:08:24,960 --> 00:08:26,880
 - And we see that in the market.

218
00:08:26,880 --> 00:08:28,720
 Like I spent a lot of them talking to Fintechs to say,

219
00:08:28,720 --> 00:08:32,000
 how are you looking to expand your payment capabilities?

220
00:08:32,000 --> 00:08:33,640
 And a lot of them are saying stablecoins

221
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:37,640
 because now instead of them having to go to 190 countries

222
00:08:37,640 --> 00:08:40,760
 and run a complicated regulatory complex,

