00:18:39,040 --> 00:18:41,000
 Because you're just gonna do what everyone does

525
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:41,840
 when they build a network.

526
00:18:41,840 --> 00:18:42,840
 They'll start with low fees,

527
00:18:42,840 --> 00:18:45,440
 and then they'll raise the fees as they gain power, right?

528
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:47,760
 And you can try the non-profit thing,

529
00:18:47,760 --> 00:18:49,640
 but of course, we've seen many cases

530
00:18:49,640 --> 00:18:51,120
 where non-profits flip to for-profits.

531
00:18:51,120 --> 00:18:52,320
 That doesn't really mean much.

532
00:18:52,320 --> 00:18:53,520
 The other challenge is,

533
00:18:53,520 --> 00:18:55,960
 if you defang the intermediary,

534
00:18:55,960 --> 00:18:57,200
 corporate intermediary too much,

535
00:18:57,200 --> 00:18:58,680
 then how do they go hire people?

536
00:18:58,680 --> 00:19:00,760
 How do they, like, they need to themselves become valuable

537
00:19:00,760 --> 00:19:02,680
 to recruit people, and build a company,

538
00:19:02,680 --> 00:19:04,120
 and build great technology?

539
00:19:04,120 --> 00:19:05,440
 Right, if they don't extract,

540
00:19:05,440 --> 00:19:06,600
 their lunch is gonna get eaten

541
00:19:06,600 --> 00:19:09,320
 by another corporation that does extract.

542
00:19:09,320 --> 00:19:10,160
 Yeah, that's right.

543
00:19:10,160 --> 00:19:11,800
 The competitive dynamics of tech

544
00:19:11,800 --> 00:19:13,400
 are such that it's very hard,

545
00:19:13,400 --> 00:19:15,240
 like, just as is how it works.

546
00:19:15,240 --> 00:19:17,040
 So, blockchains are really powerful

547
00:19:17,040 --> 00:19:18,720
 in that they kind of are the best of both worlds.

548
00:19:18,720 --> 00:19:20,200
 You can build stateful protocols,

549
00:19:20,200 --> 00:19:21,160
 you can keep a ledger,

550
00:19:21,160 --> 00:19:22,400
 you can do all these powerful things

551
00:19:22,400 --> 00:19:23,920
 you wanna do with payments,

552
00:19:23,920 --> 00:19:25,160
 but you can do it in a way

553
00:19:25,160 --> 00:19:28,000
 that there is no central, powerful intermediary.

554
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:30,120
 Architecturally, that's the core innovation of a blockchain,

555
00:19:30,120 --> 00:19:33,440
 is that you have these so-called consensus mechanism,

556
00:19:33,440 --> 00:19:35,880
 game theoretic mechanism pioneered by Bitcoin

557
00:19:35,880 --> 00:19:38,240
 and expanded on by later blockchains.

558
00:19:38,240 --> 00:19:39,320
 And I think about what Bitcoin is.

559
00:19:39,320 --> 00:19:41,600
 Bitcoin, what it essentially is, right,

560
00:19:41,600 --> 00:19:42,840
 is it's a centralized ledger.

561
00:19:42,840 --> 00:19:45,720
 It keeps track of who has how many Bitcoins,

562
00:19:45,720 --> 00:19:47,880
 but it does it in a way that there's no Bitcoin company.

563
00:19:47,880 --> 00:19:49,000
 There's no one behind it, right?

564
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:50,440
 It's a group of people coming together

565
00:19:50,440 --> 00:19:52,280
 using this kind of game theoretic mechanism

566
00:19:52,280 --> 00:19:54,360
 that provides the core service

567
00:19:54,360 --> 00:19:56,680
 of a financial network, a payment network,

568
00:19:56,680 --> 00:19:57,800
 without any intermediary.

569
00:19:57,800 --> 00:20:00,880
 Now it has pros and cons and its architecture and fees,

570
00:20:00,880 --> 00:20:02,680
 and you can have a whole debate about it.

571
00:20:02,680 --> 00:20:04,040
 There's been a lot of work since then,

572
00:20:04,040 --> 00:20:05,840
 and specifically in what we will call

573
00:20:05,840 --> 00:20:08,680
 the programmable blockchain world of like Ethereum and Solana,

574
00:20:08,680 --> 00:20:10,680
 to kind of expand on that, reduce the fees,

575
00:20:10,680 --> 00:20:13,240
 improve the functionality, performance reliability,

576
00:20:13,240 --> 00:20:14,200
 and so forth.

577
00:20:14,200 --> 00:20:15,680
 And that's gotten us to a point now,

578
00:20:15,680 --> 00:20:17,040
 which as of a year and a half ago,

579
00:20:17,040 --> 00:20:19,840
 Sam was saying we've now had an important milestone

580
00:20:19,840 --> 00:20:22,680
 of you can now send stablecoins under one second

581
00:20:22,680 --> 00:20:23,880
 and under one penny.

582
00:20:23,880 --> 00:20:25,760
 And by the way, this is not the theoretical download

583
00:20:25,760 --> 00:20:28,040
 coin-based wallet, and then try it.

584
00:20:28,040 --> 00:20:30,400
 I just tried it yesterday to double confirm it.

585
00:20:30,400 --> 00:20:32,520
 - I've got a fun, a fun story for you about this.

586
00:20:32,520 --> 00:20:33,640
 I was playing basketball the other day.

587
00:20:33,640 --> 00:20:34,680
 I rent out this gym,

588
00:20:34,680 --> 00:20:36,520
 and so I collect payment from everyone at the end of the day.

589
00:20:36,520 --> 00:20:37,680
 And this guy comes up to me and he's like,

590
00:20:37,680 --> 00:20:39,320
 hey, you're saying, I see you on Twitter.

591
00:20:39,320 --> 00:20:40,160
 You love stablecoins.

592
00:20:40,160 --> 00:20:42,400
 So he says, what do I pay you in USDC?

593
00:20:42,400 --> 00:20:43,400
 So yeah, it's 20 bucks.

594
00:20:43,400 --> 00:20:46,200
 Here's my ETH address, so I'm SamBroner. ETH.

595
00:20:46,200 --> 00:20:50,200
 He sends me 20 bucks, costs like a small fraction of a cent.

596
00:20:50,200 --> 00:20:54,040
 And I then put that into a lending protocol.

597
00:20:54,040 --> 00:20:55,640
 So I'm like earning yield on it.

598
00:20:55,640 --> 00:20:57,680
 And about 24 hours later,

599
00:20:57,680 --> 00:21:01,200
 I've made enough in interest that I paid back the fee.

600
00:21:01,200 --> 00:21:03,600
 Now, like what a crazy dynamic that is.

601
00:21:03,600 --> 00:21:06,680
 Like, I mean, now I've made more than 20 bucks.

602
00:21:06,680 --> 00:21:09,360
 If that was in Venmo, I wanted the money right away.

603
00:21:09,360 --> 00:21:11,280
 It would have taken me three days to get it out of Venmo,

604
00:21:11,280 --> 00:21:13,080
 let alone like the nonsense interest rate

605
00:21:13,080 --> 00:21:14,240
 I'm getting in my bank.

606
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:16,400
 So I get the money in a second.

607
00:21:16,400 --> 00:21:18,280
 It's mine. I can send it to someone else.

608
00:21:18,280 --> 00:21:20,040
 Or I can sweep as in and just like,

609
00:21:20,040 --> 00:21:22,280
 put that money into saying that's earning interest.

610
00:21:22,280 --> 00:21:24,640
 And suddenly I'm out on top of games on Saturday

611
00:21:24,640 --> 00:21:26,520
 by like Monday afternoon.

612
00:21:26,520 --> 00:21:27,600
 I've made my money back.

613
00:21:27,600 --> 00:21:30,720
 And you just can't do that on normal rails at all.

614
00:21:30,720 --> 00:21:31,560
 - That's really interesting

615
00:21:31,560 --> 00:21:33,680
 when you consider a lot of FinTech business models.

616
00:21:33,680 --> 00:21:35,200
 I mean, you mentioned Venmo.

617
00:21:35,200 --> 00:21:36,720
 They're just banking on a lot of people

618
00:21:36,720 --> 00:21:38,400
 leaving money in their accounts.

619
00:21:38,400 --> 00:21:40,880
 And then they get to collect interest on them.

620
00:21:40,880 --> 00:21:41,720
 - Yeah.

621
00:21:41,720 --> 00:21:42,640
 - But you're able to actually earn interest

622
00:21:42,640 --> 00:21:43,980
 on the stablecoins that you were sent.

623
00:21:43,980 --> 00:21:45,520
 - Yeah, yeah, absolutely.

624
00:21:45,520 --> 00:21:48,200
 So look, these institutions, they money in a couple of ways.

625
00:21:48,200 --> 00:21:51,400
 That interest, we call it Nim, this net interest margin.

626
00:21:51,400 --> 00:21:52,240
 There's other ways to get paid.

627
00:21:52,240 --> 00:21:55,040
 Like they'll also charge you when you try to take the money out.

628
00:21:55,040 --> 00:21:57,960
 And so like, if you try to withdraw money from Venmo

629
00:21:57,960 --> 00:22:01,040
 immediately rather than waiting the normal three or four days,

630
00:22:01,040 --> 00:22:03,040
 they'll also charge you 3% there.

631
00:22:03,040 --> 00:22:05,360
 And so no matter how you slice it,

632
00:22:05,360 --> 00:22:07,280
 you're getting a pretty raw deal.

633
00:22:07,280 --> 00:22:11,640
 What is so interesting is that because only certain institutions

634
00:22:11,640 --> 00:22:13,040
 have permission from the government,

635
00:22:13,040 --> 00:22:16,120
 they have regulatory lock-in to hold your money,

636
00:22:16,120 --> 00:22:18,440
 there's not a very competitive market here.

637
00:22:18,440 --> 00:22:21,400
 We should all be getting 4% interest on our savings.

638
00:22:21,400 --> 00:22:24,000
 That's a little bit better than inflation.

639
00:22:24,000 --> 00:22:27,720
 And we should benefit from the sort of growth of the economy.

640
00:22:27,720 --> 00:22:29,240
 That's how the world should work.

641
00:22:29,240 --> 00:22:33,120
 Instead, Bank of America or Venmo or PayPal

642
00:22:33,120 --> 00:22:34,960
 is sitting on your money and giving you nothing

643
00:22:34,960 --> 00:22:36,760
 while they take that home for,

644
00:22:36,760 --> 00:22:37,920
 "Look, they've got a nice product,

645
00:22:37,920 --> 00:22:39,800
 but I think we could recreate in a weekend

646
00:22:39,800 --> 00:22:43,480
 what we couldn't recreate is the network effects that they have.

647
00:22:43,480 --> 00:22:45,720
 And so they get to be extracted with those network effects.

648
00:22:45,720 --> 00:22:47,280
 And that's what they've done to date.

649
00:22:47,280 --> 00:22:50,080
 But there is a better way like this basketball scenario

650
00:22:50,080 --> 00:22:51,880
 where I get the money on day one,

651
00:22:51,880 --> 00:22:53,400
 and then I get interest as well.

652
00:22:53,400 --> 00:22:56,200
 - It reminds me of AOL, for the longest time,

653
00:22:56,200 --> 00:22:58,840
 was just collecting fees from zombie accounts.

654
00:22:58,840 --> 00:23:01,640
 People who hadn't actually severed their relationship.

655
00:23:01,640 --> 00:23:03,760
 They're just paying subscriptions every year,

656
00:23:03,760 --> 00:23:06,120
 thinking like that's how they get internet access,

657
00:23:06,120 --> 00:23:07,520
 just making lots of money off this,

658
00:23:07,520 --> 00:23:10,280
 well beyond the point when you need to actually pay

659
00:23:10,280 --> 00:23:13,200
 for that subscription to get internet access.

660
00:23:13,200 --> 00:23:15,920
 So how long is that interest generating business model

661
00:23:15,920 --> 00:23:17,960
 going to be sustaining these FinTechs?

662
00:23:17,960 --> 00:23:20,720
 - So look, we're seeing the first use cases

663
00:23:20,720 --> 00:23:23,920
 of stablecoin payments happen in the wild right now.

664
00:23:23,920 --> 00:23:26,600
 And it's different by geography and by user.

665
00:23:26,600 --> 00:23:28,680
 In the US for wealthy users,

666
00:23:28,680 --> 00:23:30,480
 they're thinking my credit card rocks.

667
00:23:30,480 --> 00:23:31,920
 I get 3% off everything.

668
00:23:31,920 --> 00:23:33,000
 It's accepted everywhere.

669
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:34,600
 I don't really need to change.

670
00:23:34,600 --> 00:23:37,240
 But the PSPs, so Stripe,

671
00:23:37,240 --> 00:23:40,000
 whoever runs sort of the payment terminal at a coffee shop,

672
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:41,920
 these guys are getting like 10 basis points

673
00:23:41,920 --> 00:23:43,200
 and they're actually giving most of the money

674
00:23:43,200 --> 00:23:44,800
 to the issuing banks and Visa.

675
00:23:44,800 --> 00:23:48,080
 And so those payment service providers,

676
00:23:48,080 --> 00:23:51,200
 they have an incentive to switch over to crypto rails

677
00:23:51,200 --> 00:23:53,520
 'cause they can offer users a better product

678
00:23:53,520 --> 00:23:55,840
 while taking maybe 10 X more fees.

679
00:23:55,840 --> 00:24:00,840
 Like they could charge 0.5% instead of 0.1%

680
00:24:00,840 --> 00:24:04,120
 while still offering the merchant a better deal.

681
00:24:04,120 --> 00:24:06,880
 So that dynamic will encourage payment service providers

682
00:24:06,880 --> 00:24:07,840
 in the US to switch.

683
00:24:07,840 --> 00:24:10,040
 - They can raise their fees, get more money

684
00:24:10,040 --> 00:24:12,520
 and yet still drop the cost for customers.

685
00:24:12,520 --> 00:24:13,760
 - 'Cause they're cutting out the intermediary

686
00:24:13,760 --> 00:24:14,880
 and it's an unbelievable opportunity.

687
00:24:14,880 --> 00:24:17,080
 We've seen Stripe start to go in this direction.

688
00:24:17,080 --> 00:24:18,680
 So they're the first to turn it on

689
00:24:18,680 --> 00:24:20,400
 so they get this first mover advantage.

690
00:24:20,400 --> 00:24:24,040
 They're charging 1.5% for stablecoin payments.

691
00:24:24,040 --> 00:24:24,880
 That's pretty high.

692
00:24:24,880 --> 00:24:29,560
 I think they're probably making 1% on every transaction

693
00:24:29,560 --> 00:24:32,000
 but it's still a great deal for merchants

694
00:24:32,000 --> 00:24:34,200
 because merchants are used to paying 3%.

695
00:24:34,200 --> 00:24:35,800
 The merchants love it and Stripe loves it

696
00:24:35,800 --> 00:24:38,000
 'cause they take home a lot more money at the end of the day.

697
00:24:38,000 --> 00:24:40,680
 So in the US, I think that's where we'll first see it

698
00:24:40,680 --> 00:24:42,080
 where users aren't gonna realize

699
00:24:42,080 --> 00:24:43,600
 that they're using crypto necessarily

700
00:24:43,600 --> 00:24:44,800
 but the payment service providers

701
00:24:44,800 --> 00:24:46,960
 are gonna be really pushing this new angle.

702
00:24:46,960 --> 00:24:48,680
 In lower income or high inflation countries,

703
00:24:48,680 --> 00:24:50,360
 people actually wanna hold stablecoins directly.

704
00:24:50,360 --> 00:24:51,520
 They'll interact with it.

705
00:24:51,520 --> 00:24:52,960
 They'll pay with it.

706
00:24:52,960 --> 00:24:55,640
 We do have a ways to go though

707
00:24:55,640 --> 00:24:58,840
 until everyone feels really comfortable with stablecoins.

708
00:24:58,840 --> 00:25:01,960
 We need to create what some people call a stablecoin mullet.

709
00:25:01,960 --> 00:25:03,080
 It's stablecoins in the back

710
00:25:03,080 --> 00:25:04,840
 but it looks like you're used to in the front

711
00:25:04,840 --> 00:25:08,400
 and people just get cheaper products that work better

712
00:25:08,400 --> 00:25:11,080
 but they integrate where users already are.

713
00:25:11,080 --> 00:25:13,880
 Another example just to linger on this for a second is Skype.

714
00:25:13,880 --> 00:25:15,840
 People think of it as sort of video conferencing

715
00:25:15,840 --> 00:25:16,920
 but when it first got started,

716
00:25:16,920 --> 00:25:18,920
 one of the key features they enabled

717
00:25:18,920 --> 00:25:20,720
 is that from your computer,

718
00:25:20,720 --> 00:25:22,720
 you could call someone's landline.

719
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:25,520
 And so that interoperability

720
00:25:25,520 --> 00:25:28,240
 is part of what created the onboarding.

721
00:25:28,240 --> 00:25:30,360
 Stablecoins probably need to have that moment

722
00:25:30,360 --> 00:25:33,720
 where from my wallet that holds stablecoins,

723
00:25:33,720 --> 00:25:36,040
 I can hit anyone else's account

724
00:25:36,040 --> 00:25:38,640
 no matter what denomination or technology it is.

725
00:25:38,640 --> 00:25:39,840
 That backwards could be huge.

726
00:25:39,840 --> 00:25:41,480
 And then eventually we're gonna end up with WhatsApp

727
00:25:41,480 --> 00:25:44,240
 and FaceTime and things like once people fully get oriented

728
00:25:44,240 --> 00:25:45,360
 to this new world,

729
00:25:45,360 --> 00:25:47,040
 but we need to have Skype-like technology

730
00:25:47,040 --> 00:25:48,880
 to integrate and be backwards compatible.

731
00:25:48,880 --> 00:25:51,360
 - How do you achieve that backwards compatibility?

732
00:25:51,360 --> 00:25:53,720
 I mean, if we're talking about how stablecoins

733
00:25:53,720 --> 00:25:55,760
 and blockchains disintermediate,

734
00:25:55,760 --> 00:25:59,240
 how are you gonna get existing players on board with that?

735
00:25:59,240 --> 00:26:01,040
 - One of the things that we think about the most

736
00:26:01,040 --> 00:26:04,320
 on the investing side is who is really gonna help us,

737
00:26:04,320 --> 00:26:07,680
 help the world move forward with this new technology?

738
00:26:07,680 --> 00:26:09,120
 The banks probably aren't gonna do it.

739
00:26:09,120 --> 00:26:10,760
 They love the current situation.

740
00:26:10,760 --> 00:26:13,000
 They're very stagnant.

741
00:26:13,000 --> 00:26:15,160
 The FinTechs are extremely excited.

742
00:26:15,160 --> 00:26:17,440
 They think, wow, I can go to market in 190 countries.

743
00:26:17,440 --> 00:26:19,720
 I can be universally on everywhere.

744
00:26:19,720 --> 00:26:21,600
 I can work with service providers,

745
00:26:21,600 --> 00:26:23,600
 basically entrepreneurs who are building functionality.

746
00:26:23,600 --> 00:26:24,400
 I don't wanna deal with.

747
00:26:24,400 --> 00:26:25,600
 I wanna focus on my core product

748
00:26:25,600 --> 00:26:28,320
 and let them move the rest forward.

749
00:26:28,320 --> 00:26:30,080
 There are things also that we need to build

750
00:26:30,080 --> 00:26:34,800
 on top of stablecoins to reach table stakes payments capability.

751
00:26:34,800 --> 00:26:38,120
 So Chris mentioned chargebacks earlier,

752
00:26:38,120 --> 00:26:43,120
 but like we also need escrow subscriptions, refunds.

753
00:26:43,120 --> 00:26:46,320
 We need vaulting, which is where you store

754
00:26:46,320 --> 00:26:47,720
 a payment credential on a website.

755
00:26:47,720 --> 00:26:50,160
 Like when you go to Amazon, the credit card's already there.

756
00:26:50,160 --> 00:26:51,040
 We need promotions.

757
00:26:51,040 --> 00:26:52,840
 We need people to be able to easily discount.

758
00:26:52,840 --> 00:26:55,680
 We need integration into ERPs,

759
00:26:55,680 --> 00:26:57,720
 enterprise resource planning tools

760
00:26:57,720 --> 00:27:00,520
 so that the CFO can look at the state of their business

761
00:27:00,520 --> 00:27:02,240
 and say, I've got this many stablecoins.

762
00:27:02,240 --> 00:27:04,480
 I'm gonna pay my supplier tomorrow.

763
00:27:04,480 --> 00:27:06,640
 They're gonna get it in exchange for this invoice.

764
00:27:06,640 --> 00:27:08,800
 Those are the integrations that need to be built

765
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:10,120
 if we wanna reach mass market.

766
00:27:10,120 --> 00:27:12,440
 And there's a huge opportunities

767
00:27:12,440 --> 00:27:14,480
 in each of those capabilities

768
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:18,480
 for people to reach traditional payment rail parity.

769
00:27:18,480 --> 00:27:21,360
 There's another issue that I love talking about

770
00:27:21,360 --> 00:27:25,120
 'cause I was briefly working at the Boston Federal Reserve Bank,

771
00:27:25,120 --> 00:27:26,680
 but the singleness of money,

772
00:27:26,680 --> 00:27:30,520
 which is that I trust that a dollar in my wallet

773
00:27:30,520 --> 00:27:32,760
 is the same as a dollar in your bank account

774
00:27:32,760 --> 00:27:35,520
 and is worth a dollar in Chris's Venmo.

775
00:27:35,520 --> 00:27:38,760
 That singleness of money took a long time to develop.

776
00:27:38,760 --> 00:27:41,560
 I wrote a piece on the history of banking

777
00:27:41,560 --> 00:27:43,000
 and how that relates to stablecoins.

778
00:27:43,000 --> 00:27:47,040
 And before 1910, you really weren't positive

779
00:27:47,040 --> 00:27:49,160
 that a dollar bill in your hand was worth a dollar bill

780
00:27:49,160 --> 00:27:52,520
 in your hand 'cause you had to trust the credit worthiness

781
00:27:52,520 --> 00:27:54,280
 of the bank that it was associated with.

782
00:27:54,280 --> 00:27:56,920
 These were bank-issued dollars.

783
00:27:56,920 --> 00:28:01,480
 And today, through a huge amount of regulation,

784
00:28:01,480 --> 00:28:04,640
 but also sophisticated risk management, interoperability,

